I thought the lance could only break during a "joust", and that a
joust only happens for an adjacent monster, not one 2 squares away?
I agree though that pounding with the lance from 2 squares away is a
nice way to prevent yourself from getting hurt.

On the whole, I found the lance only mildly useful during my knight
ascension.  I generally felt happier twoweaponing Grayswandir and a
silver saber rather than using a lance.  I think you may as well try
it, though.
One nuisance is that the lance is heavy.  It's nice to have an
expertly skilled +7 polearm to kill krakens or Croesus from a
distance, but the weight 180 is annoying.

Riding on the other hand _is_ a game changer.  Warhorses are twice as
fast as you are and can be hit with a wand of speed monster for extra
speed.  They're annoying to get across water without a magic whistle,
though.  If you want to trade up mounts, you can #loot your current
one for its saddle.  Don't lead a horse into water.
Once you have magic resistance and reflection AND you have a spare
amulet of reflection, I would absolutely spend a wish on a blessed
figurine of a ki-rin.  Keep displacing it onto the "oR until it picks
it up and puts it on, let it level up to 192 HP, and you'll generally
not need to worry about its health unless you get badly surrounded
(like on the astral plane).  Ki-rins fly over water.  They fly over
spiked pits.  They heal themselves.  and they're relatively fast.
Ever since I played that knight, I've been wishing for a saddle and a
ki-rin with every class that can get to basic in riding.  If you're
playing a knight that can dismount your ki-rin whenever you feel like
it, you definitely want to ride one.

Regarding the knight's code of conduct, I still don't feel like the
penalty matters.  Killing monsters raises your alignment faster than
bad conduct can drop it.  I suppose finding a throne room full of
sleeping lawful monsters could temporarily hurt your alignment, but it
would return fast enough.


